      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your critique touches on a significant issue known as \"aesthetic silence,\" where the lack of skill in execution undermines the power of the political message. Art historians often argue that true critical success happens when a work forces viewers to think *about* the politics rather than simply *agree* with them, distinguishing deep engagement from mere virtue signaling. If a piece relies entirely on the viewer's pre-existing ideology to make sense, many would indeed agree it operates more like political propaganda than high art, as it fails to establish its own internal logic and aesthetic weight.",
